A||||3||theogre||Cave Update. Brakes. ALL READ!||||10||
Z||000000||theogre||08-25-2001||12:43 PM||||Once again the cave has been updated. There are a few minor updates and one very major one.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> There is now a procedure, linked off the brake service article, for a <u>reliable</u> way to <u>manually</u> adjust the rear brakes. <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_brow.gif">I do not know if this procedure will work on 88 rears. I think it will but have no way to test it. If you do this on an 88 be careful and please report the result to this thread.<P>Some of you may be aware that excessive rear caliper clearance is a pretty big problem. Just how big a problem is it? It is a major cause of performance problems in Fiero brakes. This single factor has a dramatic effect on stopping distance. It’s also a very major cause of low pedal problems in Fiero and many other GM all wheel disk setups.<P>Low Pedal is when the brakes work properly except the pedal travel is longer than normal. As opposed to Spongy Pedal that is nearly always a bleeding issue. When Fiero brakes are setup and working like they should be, the pedal will “go solid” before reaching the same plane as the gas pedal resting position. <P>The brake service article now includes a reliable way to manually adjust the OE rear calipers to a repeatable clearance. This procedure should be used anytime the rear calipers are serviced or if you have low pedal problems. (Do this before you mess with the MC or Booster when dealing with low pedal.)<P>Warning! The brakes WILL perform differently after doing the adjustment procedure! Just how much different depends on how far off they were to begin with and condition of the rest of the system.<P>While developing the procedure, I tested it on my car. The brake pedal came up around an inch, maybe more. It took me several days to get used to the new pedal height and system performance.<P>I believe this problem affects nearly all 84-87 Fiero and maybe 88’s. Incorrect rear caliper clearance is likely the #1 cause of poor performance of otherwise good brake systems. I was amazed how much difference the manual adjustment made to my car.<P><BR>------------------<BR><I><B>Screamin Yellow Zonkers... If it's Screamin and Yellow, I ain't eatin it.</B></I><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-25-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000001||Primaris||08-25-2001||01:11 PM||||Orge,<BR>I'm using IE 5.5 and the frame on the left side of the screen is too narrow.  It needs to be moved over about 1cm.  There is no way for me to make that frame larger in my browser.  Some of your dropdown boxes are almost completely hidden.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||08-25-2001||01:31 PM||||The drop buttons on the end of the boxes are not essential. just click in the text box and they should open. There should be a scroll on the bottom of the frame if you need it. <P>I run IE5.5 on several machines and it works ok on them all.<P>I set the frame like that so it wouldn't fill smaller screens. There area still allot fo people runnning 15" monitors around and many of them don't even know you can size a frame.<P>I'll fool with it next week and see if I can make it more flexible. I don't particularly want a frame margin. I don't think I can make is sizeable w/o one. ||||reg||1
